# A novel ADP- and zinc-binding fold from function-directed in vitro evolution

## abstract
A great challenge to biologists is to create proteins with novel folds and tailored functions. As an alternative to de novo protein design, we investigated the structure of a randomly generated protein targeted to bind ATP. The crystal structure reveals a novel alpha/beta fold bound to its ligand, representing both the first protein structure derived from in vitro evolution and the first nucleotide-binding protein stabilized by a zinc ion.

Paola Lo Surdo1, Martin A Walsh2 & Maurizio Sollazzo1
A great challenge to biologists is to create proteins with novel folds and tailored functions. As an alternative to de novo protein design, we investigated the structure of a randomly generated protein targeted to bind ATP. The crystal structure reveals a novel $\mathbf{\alpha}\mathbf{\alpha}\mathbf{\alpha}\mathbf{\beta}$ fold bound to its ligand, representing both the first protein structure derived from in vitro evolution and the first nucleotide-binding protein stabilized by a zinc ion.
There are two main reasons to pursue protein design. The first is academic: to test our knowledge of basic principles, because an understanding of any structural phenomenon is demonstrated only by the design of a similar structure from first principles. The second is of broad general interest: attempts at protein design are the first steps toward tailor-made proteins with the potential to solve important chemical, biochemical or therapeutic issues. These motives have driven much research and protein designers have achieved landmark successes in recent years. Novel proteins have been designed computationally to form predominantly $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{\upalpha}}^{1}$ , predominantly $\upbeta^{2-3}$ or mixed structures, such as a remodeled $\upalpha/\upbeta$ zinc finger protein4. Although some of these novel proteins are structurally related to pre-existing folds, Baker and co-workers have recently designed a completely novel, stable globular fold that was also experimentally shown to closely resemble their design model5. Despite these advances, the rational design of well-folded proteins is not straightforward, largely because it demands both fine optimization of hydrophobic packing and minimization of the backbone configurational entropy of unfolded states6. Harder still is the design of folded proteins that also have tailored functions. Is there hope that alternative routes to novel functional proteins could bypass the complex issues of design? One possibility is provided by ‘intelligent selection’ methods like those using directed evolution or randomized protein-display libraries3. Given the appeal of such alternatives, we studied whether a randomly generated protein with a tailored function (by necessity produced under the dictates of polymer physics but only artificially subjected to the ‘selective laws of nature’) would exhibit the structural and biophysical characteristics of natural proteins.
Our investigations focused on a functional protein originally isolated, by cycles of in vitro evolutionary selection, from a randomsequence library of $6\times10^{12}$ mRNA-displayed proteins, as described recently7. Notably, Keefe and Szostak7 directed the evolution of an 80-residue protein with tailored ATP- and ADP-binding functions $(K_{\mathrm{d}}\sim100\ n M)$ ). Although the protein has no apparent sequence homologs, its topological novelty remained unclear because its structural features were uncharacterized. We sought to determine whether this protein has the same features as natural proteins and to establish whether a new protein fold could be generated by such an approach. Here we present the high-resolution crystal structure of this protein (hereafter called ANBP, artificial nucleotide-binding protein) in complex with its ligand. The ANBP structure is a novel
ADP- and zinc-binding fold, and, to our knowledge, it also represents the first structure of a protein derived from in vitro evolution.
Although an evolutionary relationship of ANBP to natural proteins is precluded, the ligand-binding site shows features typical of adeninebinding proteins9. The adenine and ribose moieties of ADP are bound by aromatic side chains in a notable stacking interaction, with additional hydrogen bonding interactions between the ADP phosphate groups and polar side chains (Fig. 1b). Scrutiny of the binding site provides an atomic understanding of the decrease in binding affinities reported for related but distinct ligands such as AMP, adenosine, GTP, CTP or base-substituted analogs (such as 2-chloro ATP)7 (Fig. 1c). In short, the crystal structure reveals the inextricable link between fold and function of this novel nucleotide-binding domain.
The ANBP structure has many features characteristic of natural, independently folding domains: it has multiple secondary structure elements in close contact; in $^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR solution spectra $(600\mathrm{MHz})$ it shows notable chemical shift dispersion, reflecting its tertiary interactions; it is considerably resistant to thermal unfolding; and Ramachandran analyses show both an excellent $\upphi/\uppsi$ angle distribution and well-defined $\boldsymbol{\chi}$ angle values resulting from favorable side chain packing interactions. It had been previously suspected that the probability of a stable fold arising at random is extremely small (argument based on the complexity paradox that the disparity between the many potential protein sequences and the relatively few different structures known). However, we have confirmed that functionally directed in vitro evolution from random sequences can generate a novel fold with a tailored function. In addition, such folds may display the key features of naturally evolving proteins.
We conclude that function-directed in vitro evolution is a powerful new method for generating protein structures with tailored functions. Moreover, we demonstrate that this process can produce novel protein
protein side chains (gray) and the ADP ligand (black). Many intermolecular hydrogen bonds are formed, connecting (i) the ADP phosphate groups with the side chains of $\mathsf{A r g41}$ and Tyr43, (ii) the ribose unit with Gly63 and His64 and (iii) the adenine moiety with Met45 and Gly63. From the ligand, hydrogen bonds are formed by the N1, N3 and N6 adenine groups, as seen in a large group of adenine-binding protein complexes9. The coordinates and structural factors have been deposited in the Protein Data Bank (accession code 1UW1).
folds with the same characteristics as natural protein domains. In short, functional new topologies can be derived without resorting to complex protein design methodology. These findings not only are of academic interest but also have potential applications in many fields, including diagnostics for molecular medicine, biosensors and nanotechnology, in which the production of tailored, high-specificity molecular recognition modules is of great importance10.
